ce of a prefilled syringe for both products","explanation":"A shared delivery device does not establish equivalent doses."},{"id":"B","text":"Product-specific strength units and prescribed regimen","explanation":"Related LMWHs are not automatically dose-equivalent."},{"id":"C","text":"The presence of antithrombin-mediated effects in both","explanation":"A shared broad mechanism does not establish equivalent doses."},{"id":"D","text":"The use of the subcutaneous route by both products","explanation":"A shared route does not establish dose equivalence."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Nadroparin and enoxaparin are different LMWH products.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-nadroparin--7-day--lmwh_substitution","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-nadroparin::lmwh_substitution"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-nadroparin--advanced--lmwh_substitution","answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":8}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ccu-enoxaparin","title":"CLEXANE / CLEXAN — enoxaparin","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-enoxaparin--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-enoxaparin","itemType":"medication","title":"CLEXANE / CLEXAN — enoxaparin","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of CLEXANE / CLEXAN — enoxaparin?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1","explanation":"This describes Antiplatelet. LMWH activity is predominantly anti-Xa; different products are not dose-for-dose interchangeable."},{"id":"B","text":"Enhances antithrombin activity, predominantly against Xa","explanation":"LMWH activity is predominantly anti-Xa; different products are not dose-for-dose interchangeable."},{"id":"C","text":"Irreversibly inhibits platelet P2Y12 receptors","explanation":"This describes Antiplatelet. LMWH activity is predominantly anti-Xa; different products are not dose-for-dose interchangeable."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduces vitamin-K-dependent factor synthesis","explanation":"This describes Vitamin-K antagonist. LMWH activity is predominantly anti-Xa; different products are not dose-for-dose interchangeable."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-enoxaparin--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-enoxaparin::principal-action"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does CLEXANE / CLEXAN — enoxaparin belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Antiplatelet","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) anticoagulant."},{"id":"B","text":"Fibrinolytic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) anticoagulant."},{"id":"C","text":"Vitamin-K antagonist","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) anticoagulant."},{"id":"D","text":"LMWH","explanation":"Low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) anticoagulant."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-enoxaparin--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-enoxaparin::drug-class"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of CLEXANE / CLEXAN — enoxaparin?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Prescribed anticoagulation in ACS or thromboembolic disease","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Prevention or treatment of venous thromboembolism and anticoagulation in selected ACS regimens. Follow the indication-specific order and protocol. Some STEMI regimens include an ordered initial IV bolus."},{"id":"B","text":"Relief of bronchospasm through airway receptor stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Suppression of bacterial protein synthesis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-enoxaparin--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-enoxaparin::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"During CLEXANE / CLEXAN — enoxaparin therapy, which blood-count change requires particular review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A progressive neutrophil fall","explanation":"Neutropenia warrants assessment, but the heparin-associated blood-count concern is the platelet trend."},{"id":"B","text":"A gradual hemoglobin rise","explanation":"A rising hemoglobin does not indicate the platelet-related complication."},{"id":"C","text":"A significant platelet fall","explanation":"Thrombocytopenia during heparin-family exposure can signal an important adverse reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"A rising eosinophil count","explanation":"This is not the platelet fall associated with heparin-related complications."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A significant platelet fall during LMWH therapy needs assessment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-enoxaparin--comprehensive--platelets","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-enoxaparin::platelets"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receives therapeutic enoxaparin. Creatinine clearance falls to 24 mL/min and the order has not changed. What should the nurse clarify before the next dose?","choices":[{"text":"Whether the dose and interval need adjustment for increased exposure.","explanation":"Below 30 mL/min, enoxaparin exposure rises and the regimen needs review.","id":"A"},{"text":"Whether the dose needs to rise because renal clearance has slowed.","explanation":"Slower clearance can increase exposure at the same dose.","id":"B"},{"text":"Whether an INR in range confirms the current dose can continue.","explanation":"INR is not used to guide routine enoxaparin dosing.","id":"C"},{"text":"Whether a stable platelet count confirms the current dose can continue.","explanation":"Platelet counts do not establish that the renal regimen is appropriate.","id":"D"}],"point":"CrCl below 30 mL/min increases exposure and requires review of the prescribed enoxaparin regimen.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-enoxaparin--comprehensive--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-enoxaparin::renal_dose"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"The full contents of the manufacturer’s enoxaparin prefilled syringe match the prescribed abdominal SC dose. Which technique is appropriate?","choices":[{"text":"Leave the air bubble in place and massage gently after injection.","explanation":"Rubbing can increase bruising.","id":"A"},{"text":"Expel the air bubble and avoid rubbing after injection.","explanation":"Expelling the bubble from a full-dose syringe can waste some medication.","id":"B"},{"text":"Expel the air bubble and massage gently after injection.","explanation":"This can waste medication and increase bruising.","id":"C"},{"text":"Leave the air bubble in place and avoid rubbing after injection.","explanation":"This follows the instructions for the full prefilled dose and reduces bruising.","id":"D"}],"point":"For a full prescribed SC dose from a prefilled syringe, retain its air bubble and avoid rubbing.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","id":"ccu-enoxaparin--comprehensive--enoxaparin_syringe","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-enoxaparin::enoxaparin_syringe"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"short-answer","stem":"Which injection route is explicitly prohibited for CLEXANE / CLEXAN — enoxaparin? Write the route name or abbreviation.","acceptedAnswers":["IM","intramuscular","intramuscular injection"],"explanation":"Intramuscular administration is explicitly excluded because of bleeding/hematoma risk. Use only the actual product’s prescribed route.","normalization":"trim-casefold-collapse-whitespace","point":"Use prescribed heparin/LMWH routes and avoid intramuscular injection.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-enoxaparin--comprehensive--lmwh_route","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-enoxaparin::lmwh_route"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving enoxaparin develops new midline back pain and progressive leg weakness after epidural catheter removal. What should the nurse urgently raise with the team?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Expected residual epidural effect.","explanation":"Progressive weakness with new back pain should not be attributed to residual anesthesia."},{"id":"B","text":"Possible spinal or epidural hematoma.","explanation":"New back pain with progressive motor deficit requires immediate evaluation for neuraxial bleeding."},{"id":"C","text":"Post-dural puncture headache.","explanation":"This usually presents as headache and does not explain progressive leg weakness."},{"id":"D","text":"Local SC injection-site hematoma.","explanation":"A local abdominal injection reaction does not explain this neurological pattern."}],"point":"New neurological symptoms after a neuraxial procedure warrant emergency evaluation for spinal hematoma.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-enoxaparin--comprehensive--enoxaparin_neuraxial_red_flag","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-enoxaparin::enoxaparin_neuraxial_red_flag"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After starting therapeutic enoxaparin, a patient develops new hypotension and a falling hemoglobin without visible bleeding. Which concern should be raised promptly?","choices":[{"text":"Occult bleeding during anticoagulation.","explanation":"Bleeding may be internal, and the combination of hypotension and falling hemoglobin requires prompt assessment.","id":"A"},{"text":"Reduced enoxaparin absorption through the injection site.","explanation":"Reduced absorption does not explain falling hemoglobin and new hypotension.","id":"B"},{"text":"Platelet suppression as the expected therapeutic effect.","explanation":"Enoxaparin acts through antithrombin. Falling hemoglobin is not an intended effect.","id":"C"},{"text":"Expected hemodilution from the SC injection volume.","explanation":"The SC dose volume does not explain clinically important hemodilution.","id":"D"}],"point":"Monitor for bleeding on enoxaparin and flag unexplained hypotension with a falling hemoglobin.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-enoxaparin--comprehensive--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-enoxaparin::bleeding"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"stem":"A patient receiving enoxaparin is being prepared for an invasive procedure. Which medication detail is especially important to communicate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Duration of the current anticoagulant course","explanation":"The course duration provides context but does not establish the recent anticoagulant exposure before a procedure."},{"id":"B","text":"Time of the next planned routine dose","explanation":"The next scheduled dose does not establish when the patient last received anticoagulation."},{"id":"C","text":"Time and amount of the most recent dose","explanation":"The team needs recent anticoagulant exposure when planning procedural bleeding safety."},{"id":"D","text":"Total number of injections given this admission","explanation":"The total number does not replace the timing and amo