eparin_protamine"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"During HEPARIN — unfractionated heparin (UFH) therapy, a patient has new melena, pallor, dizziness, and a BP fall. Which concern best unifies the findings?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Uncomplicated postural intolerance","explanation":"This does not account for the new evidence of GI bleeding."},{"id":"B","text":"A therapeutic reduction in coagulation alone","explanation":"Bleeding with circulatory symptoms is more than evidence of the intended drug action."},{"id":"C","text":"Isolated vasodilator headache","explanation":"This does not explain melena and the systemic blood-loss pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Clinically important blood loss","explanation":"The stool finding and hemodynamic change suggest bleeding with impaired perfusion."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize important bleeding during antithrombotic treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--comprehensive--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::bleeding"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Several days into UFH therapy, the platelet count has fallen substantially and the patient has a new confirmed DVT. Which concern should be escalated promptly?","point":"An unexpected platelet fall with new thrombosis requires prompt evaluation for HIT.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Bleeding from excessive anticoagulation","explanation":"Bleeding requires assessment, but the new clot plus platelet fall specifically raises concern for HIT."},{"id":"B","text":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia with thrombosis","explanation":"A substantial fall in platelets plus new thrombosis during UFH exposure should trigger prompt HIT assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Subtherapeutic anticoagulation alone","explanation":"A new DVT may suggest insufficient anticoagulation, but it does not explain away the platelet fall."},{"id":"D","text":"A benign platelet decline during UFH therapy","explanation":"The accompanying new thrombosis makes a benign explanation unsafe to assume."}],"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--comprehensive--hit","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::hit"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient is on a therapeutic UFH infusion. Which plan checks both anticoagulant effect and an early signal of HIT?","point":"Protocol-specified coagulation monitoring and platelet surveillance answer different safety questions.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Use platelet counts for titration and reserve coagulation samples for overt bleeding.","explanation":"Platelet counts do not measure the anticoagulant effect of the infusion."},{"id":"B","text":"Use coagulation results for both titration and HIT surveillance.","explanation":"Coagulation results cannot replace platelet surveillance for suspected HIT."},{"id":"C","text":"Use hemoglobin trends for titration and platelet counts for HIT surveillance.","explanation":"Hemoglobin can help assess bleeding, but it does not replace protocol-specified coagulation testing."},{"id":"D","text":"Draw protocol-timed coagulation samples and trend the platelet count.","explanation":"Coagulation testing guides authorized titration. Platelet trends help flag possible HIT."}],"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--comprehensive--heparin_samples","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::heparin_samples"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"stem":"A prescribed drug dose per minute stays the same, but the prepared infusion concentration doubles. What happens to the required mL/hour?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"It halves","explanation":"The same drug amount is delivered in half the volume when concentration doubles."},{"id":"B","text":"It becomes four times higher","explanation":"This would cause an even larger dosing increase."},{"id":"C","text":"It doubles","explanation":"That