erates inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa.","id":"section-1-8378f484efb66b9a"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Therapeutic anticoagulation and selected prophylactic uses.","id":"section-2-12598d1c7ba42aa1"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"May be SC or IV depending on the order. Therapeutic IV infusion requires a controlled pump after dilution to the hospital-standard concentration. Do not give IM; do not confuse a treatment vial with a catheter-flush product.","id":"section-3-a26fc9fc29eadcbd"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Assess for bleeding and report suspected major bleeding promptly. Protamine sulfate reverses UFH when ordered. • For therapeutic IV UFH, obtain coagulation samples at the times specified by the authorized protocol. Follow platelet trends separately to detect possible HIT. • Report an unexpected platelet fall or new thrombosis promptly for HIT assessment. • Adjust the infusion only under an authorized protocol or order.","id":"section-4-32bb60a39a15d59e"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"UFH has a variable anticoagulant effect, so therapeutic infusions commonly need controlled delivery and lab follow-up.","id":"section-5-3caf01c2f8e42e65"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"YES. Palestinian essential-medicine data document heparin 5,000 IU/mL in a 5-mL vial.","id":"section-6-757685cc466b1cc7"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--standard","itemId":"ccu-heparin-ufh","itemType":"medication","title":"HEPARIN — unfractionated heparin (UFH)","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of HEPARIN — unfractionated heparin (UFH)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Enhances antithrombin inhibition of IIa/Xa","explanation":"UFH accelerates inhibition of thrombin and activated factor X."},{"id":"B","text":"Suppresses gastric H/K ATPase","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: UFH accelerates inhibition of thrombin and activated factor X."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduces vagal bronchoconstriction","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: UFH accelerates inhibition of thrombin and activated factor X."},{"id":"D","text":"Disrupts protein synthesis at the 30S subunit","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: UFH accelerates inhibition of thrombin and activated factor X."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::principal-action"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of HEPARIN — unfractionated heparin (UFH)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Suppression of bacterial protein synthesis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Therapeutic anticoagulation or selected prophylaxis","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Therapeutic anticoagulation and selected prophylactic uses."},{"id":"D","text":"Relief of bronchospasm through airway receptor stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Before preparing a therapeutic UFH infusion, which product distinction matters most?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Tablet coating versus capsule coating","explanation":"UFH infusion preparation is not an oral tablet/capsule task."},{"id":"B","text":"Immediate-release tablet versus modified-release tablet","explanation":"This is not the product distinction for a UFH infusion."},{"id":"C","text":"Dry-powder inhaler strength versus nebulizer strength","explanation":"These are unrelated formulation categories."},{"id":"D","text":"Treatment-vial concentration versus catheter-flush concentration","explanation":"Confusing them can create a major dosing error."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Differentiate therapeutic UFH, flush products, and authorized titration.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--standard--heparin_infusion","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::heparin_infusion"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"short-answer","stem":"Which injection route is explicitly prohibited for HEPARIN — unfractionated heparin (UFH)? Write the route name or abbreviation.","acceptedAnswers":["IM","intramuscular","intramuscular injection"],"explanation":"Intramuscular administration is explicitly excluded because of bleeding/hematoma risk. Use only the actual product’s prescribed route.","normalization":"trim-casefold-collapse-whitespace","point":"Use prescribed heparin/LMWH routes and avoid intramuscular injection.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--standard--lmwh_route","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::lmwh_route"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient develops major bleeding during a UFH infusion. Which reversal agent should the nurse anticipate the team may order?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Phytonadione","explanation":"Vitamin K is used for warfarin reversal."},{"id":"B","text":"Protamine sulfate","explanation":"Protamine neutralizes UFH."},{"id":"C","text":"Andexanet alfa","explanation":"This agent targets selected direct factor Xa inhibitors."},{"id":"D","text":"Idarucizumab","explanation":"Idarucizumab reverses dabigatran."}],"point":"Protamine sulfate reverses UFH when ordered.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--standard--heparin_protamine","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::heparin_protamine"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":5},{"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--advanced","itemId":"ccu-heparin-ufh","itemType":"medication","title":"HEPARIN — unfractionated heparin (UFH)","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"During HEPARIN — unfractionated heparin (UFH) therapy, a patient has new melena, pallor, dizziness, and a BP fall. Which concern best unifies the findings?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Clinically important blood loss","explanation":"The stool finding and hemodynamic change suggest bleeding with impaired perfusion."},{"id":"B","text":"Uncomplicated postural intolerance","explanation":"This does not account for the new evidence of GI bleeding."},{"id":"C","text":"A therapeutic reduction in coagulation alone","explanation":"Bleeding with circulatory symptoms is more than evidence of the intended drug action."},{"id":"D","text":"Isolated vasodilator headache","explanation":"This does not explain melena and the systemic blood-loss pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize important bleeding during antithrombotic treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--advanced--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::bleeding"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient receiving UFH has an unexpected substantial platelet fall. Which drug-related complication should be considered?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Opioid-related central depression","explanation":"This is not a platelet-related complication."},{"id":"B","text":"Nitrate-related vasodilation","explanation":"This does not explain a platelet fall."},{"id":"C","text":"Warfarin-related vitamin-K depletion","explanation":"This is a different anticoagulant mechanism and does not explain UFH-associated HIT."},{"id":"D","text":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","explanation":"A significant platelet fall during heparin exposure needs prompt review for HIT and other causes."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize a platelet fall during UFH exposure as a HIT warning.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--advanced--hit","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::hit"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A UFH infusion has an overdue coagulation sample; the pump is running at its prescribed rate. Which interpretation is valid?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Absence of visible bleeding establishes that the result is therapeutic","explanation":"Occult bleeding or inadequate anticoagulation can occur without visible signs."},{"id":"B","text":"The prescribed mL/hour establishes a therapeutic coagulation result","explanation":"Drug delivery and measured biological effect are different."},{"id":"C","text":"Delivery accuracy does not establish the current anticoagulant response","explanation":"Obtain and interpret the ordered laboratory result under the protocol."},{"id":"D","text":"A normal platelet count replaces the ordered coag