-induced thrombocytopenia","explanation":"A significant platelet fall during heparin exposure needs prompt review for HIT and other causes."},{"id":"B","text":"Opioid-related central depression","explanation":"This is not a platelet-related complication."},{"id":"C","text":"Warfarin-related vitamin-K depletion","explanation":"This is a different anticoagulant mechanism and does not explain UFH-associated HIT."},{"id":"D","text":"Nitrate-related vasodilation","explanation":"This does not explain a platelet fall."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize a platelet fall during UFH exposure as a HIT warning.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--7-day--hit","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::hit"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-heparin-ufh--advanced--hit","answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A UFH infusion has an overdue coagulation sample; the pump is running at its prescribed rate. Which interpretation is valid?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A normal platelet count replaces the ordered coagulation test","explanation":"Platelets and coagulation-effect monitoring serve different purposes."},{"id":"B","text":"The prescribed mL/hour establishes a therapeutic coagulation result","explanation":"Drug delivery and measured biological effect are different."},{"id":"C","text":"Delivery accuracy does not establish the current anticoagulant response","explanation":"Obtain and interpret the ordered laboratory result under the protocol."},{"id":"D","text":"Absence of visible bleeding establishes that the result is therapeutic","explanation":"Occult bleeding or inadequate anticoagulation can occur without visible signs."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use the authorized coagulation and platelet monitoring plan for UFH.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--7-day--heparin_samples","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::heparin_samples"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-heparin-ufh--advanced--heparin_samples","answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A prescribed drug dose per minute stays the same, but the prepared infusion concentration doubles. What happens to the required mL/hour?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"It becomes four times higher","explanation":"This would cause an even larger dosing increase."},{"id":"B","text":"It doubles","explanation":"That would deliver four times the prior drug amount if concentration also doubled."},{"id":"C","text":"It halves","explanation":"The same drug amount is delivered in half the volume when concentration doubles."},{"id":"D","text":"It stays the same","explanation":"The same volume at double concentration would double the delivered drug dose."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Infusion delivery depends on ordered dose and prepared concentration.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-heparin-ufh--7-day--infusion_precision","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-heparin-ufh::infusion_precision"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-heparin-ufh--advanced--infusion_precision","answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":9}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ccu-aspirin","title":"ASA / ASPIRIN — aspirin (including low-dose / “baby aspirin”)","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-aspirin--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-aspirin","itemType":"medication","title":"ASA / ASPIRIN — aspirin (including low-dose / “baby aspirin”)","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":1},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of ASA / ASPIRIN — aspirin (including low-dose / “baby aspirin”)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1","explanation":"Reduced thromboxane A2 synthesis impairs platelet aggregation."},{"id":"B","text":"Irreversibly inhibits platelet P2Y12 receptors","explanation":"This describes Antiplatelet. Reduced thromboxane A2 synthesis impairs platelet aggregation."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduces vitamin-K-dependent factor synthesis","explanation":"This describes Vitamin-K antagonist. Reduced thromboxane A2 synthesis impairs platelet aggregation."},{"id":"D","text":"Activates plasminogen to break down fibrin","explanation":"This describes Fibrinolytic. Reduced thromboxane A2 synthesis impairs platelet aggregation."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-aspirin--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-aspirin::principal-action"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does ASA / ASPIRIN — aspirin (including low-dose / “baby aspirin”) belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Fibrinolytic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Antiplatelet; irreversible platelet COX-1 inhibitor."},{"id":"B","text":"Antifibrinolytic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Antiplatelet; irreversible platelet COX-1 inhibitor."},{"id":"C","text":"Antiplatelet","explanation":"Antiplatelet; irreversible platelet COX-1 inhibitor."},{"id":"D","text":"LMWH","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Antiplatelet; irreversible platelet COX-1 inhibitor."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-aspirin--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-aspirin::drug-class"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of ASA / ASPIRIN — aspirin (including low-dose / “baby aspirin”)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Suppression of bacterial protein synthesis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Antiplatelet treatment when indicated","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Same drug at different doses. Low-dose aspirin ('baby aspirin') usually means about 75–100 mg daily and is used mainly for antiplatelet effect when indicated. Higher aspirin doses are used for analgesic/antipyretic or anti-inflammatory effects depending on the indication and product."},{"id":"C","text":"Relief of bronchospasm through airway receptor stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-aspirin--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-aspirin::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"What does the term baby aspirin mean?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A pediatric formulation of clopidogrel","explanation":"Clopidogrel is a different antiplatelet drug."},{"id":"B","text":"A modified-release form of warfarin","explanation":"Warfarin is a different anticoagulant mechanism."},{"id":"C","text":"A low-dose formulation of heparin","explanation":"Heparin is a different anticoagulant."},{"id":"D","text":"Low-dose aspirin used mainly for its antiplatelet effect","explanation":"It is the same active drug, not a separate medicine for babies."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Low-dose or baby aspirin is aspirin at a smaller dose.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-aspirin--comprehensive--aspirin_identity","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-aspirin::aspirin_identity"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which previous reaction most directly changes the hypersensitivity assessment before ASA / ASPIRIN — aspirin (including low-dose / “baby aspirin”)?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Nausea after an opioid","explanation"