--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-clopidogrel::bleeding"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"stem":"A recently stented patient has no chest pain, but clopidogrel is unexpectedly missing from today’s chart. Which issue needs immediate clarification?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Whether a normal pulse establishes that the stent needs no protection","explanation":"Pulse examination does not establish freedom from stent thrombosis risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Whether symptom relief means the loading course is complete","explanation":"Absence of pain does not establish that maintenance can stop."},{"id":"C","text":"Whether interruption was authorized given stent-thrombosis risk","explanation":"Being pain-free does not remove the indication for prescribed antiplatelet therapy."},{"id":"D","text":"Whether aspirin automatically replaces the omitted P2Y12 effect","explanation":"These antiplatelets have different targets and prescribed roles."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Unexpected clopidogrel omission after a recent stent needs clarification.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-clopidogrel--comprehensive--stent_omission","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-clopidogrel::stent_omission"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A post-PCI patient taking clopidogrel needs a PPI. Omeprazole is ordered 12 hours apart from clopidogrel. What change should the nurse discuss with the prescriber?","choices":[{"text":"Continue omeprazole at the separated time to preserve clopidogrel activation.","explanation":"Twelve-hour separation does not prevent the interaction.","id":"A"},{"text":"Use a suitable alternative such as pantoprazole instead of relying on the separation.","explanation":"Clopidogrel’s activity can be reduced even when omeprazole is taken 12 hours apart.","id":"B"},{"text":"Use esomeprazole at the separated time to preserve clopidogrel activation.","explanation":"Esomeprazole also reduces clopidogrel 