ual acuity and accommodation symptoms","explanation":"These evaluate visual effects rather than establish a postural pressure fall."},{"id":"B","text":"Resting temperature and infection symptoms","explanation":"These investigate infection rather than the suspected pressure effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Bowel frequency and abdominal distension","explanation":"These investigate a gastrointestinal effect rather than postural circulatory tolerance."},{"id":"D","text":"Postural BP and associated symptoms","explanation":"A positional pressure fall can account for the light-headedness."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize symptomatic hypotension during treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-amlodipine--7-day--hypotension","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-amlodipine::hypotension"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-amlodipine--standard--hypotension","answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which paired assessment best matches amlodipine’s important adverse-effect profile?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Blood pressure for hypotension and testing for marked potassium loss","explanation":"The BP assessment fits, but marked potassium loss is not the characteristic second effect."},{"id":"B","text":"ECG for high-grade AV block and examination for ankle edema","explanation":"This substitutes the AV-node-blocking profile of other drugs for the needed BP assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Blood pressure for hypotension and examination for ankle edema","explanation":"BP lowering can become symptomatic, and ankle edema is a relevant adverse effect."},{"id":"D","text":"Blood pressure for hypertension and examination for ankle edema","explanation":"The edema assessment fits, but the adverse pressure concern is hypotension."}],"point":"Recognize edema and BP-related adverse effects.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-amlodipine--7-day--amlodipine_bp_edema","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-amlodipine::amlodipine_bp_edema"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-amlodipine--standard--amlodipine_bp_edema","answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Ankle edema appears after AMICORE / AMICOR — amlodipine; the patient also has a history of HF. Which finding would most strengthen a concurrent congestion explanation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Unchanged breathing with stable weight and clear lungs","explanation":"This weakens, rather than establishes, a worsening congestion explanation."},{"id":"B","text":"Edema beginning after a medication change with stable breathing","explanation":"The timing supports a possible drug contribution without establishing HF."},{"id":"C","text":"New orthopnea with weight gain and crackles","explanation":"The cardiopulmonary and fluid trend supports congestion beyond ankle appearance."},{"id":"D","text":"Dependent swelling without other change in the examination","explanation":"An isolated sign is insufficient to establish total-body overload."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Peripheral edema needs clinical interpretation rather than automatic attribution to volume overload.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-amlodipine--7-day--edema","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-amlodipine::edema"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-amlodipine--advanced--edema","answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Amlodipine is prescribed for chronic hypertension. How should a high acute BP reading be interpreted in relation to this medicine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The tablet provides an immediate antimuscarinic effect","explanation":"Its action is vascular calcium-channel blockade."},{"id":"B","text":"The tablet provides an immediate fibrinolytic effect","explanation":"Amlodipine does not dissolve clots."},{"id":"C","text":"The oral tablet is equivalent to an immediately titrated IV vasodilator","explanation":"Its onset and formulation differ."},{"id":"D","text":"Acute assessment and the authorized emergency plan remain necessary","explanation":"A gradual maintenance drug does not substitute for indicated acute management."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Amlodipine is gradual chronic therapy rather than emergency BP rescue.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-amlodipine--7-day--amlodipine_onset","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-amlodipine::amlodipine_onset"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-amlodipine--advanced--amlodipine_onset","answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":7}],"scoringExclusions":[{"learningPointId":"ccu-amlodipine::hypotension","action":"omit","reason":"Context-free shared template: the answer is supplied by the symptom or asks for a general product-reference rule. Omit from drug-specific scoring; do not credit this as coverage of the underlying drug-specific administration or BP fact.","unresolvedCoverage":true}]},{"itemId":"ccu-verapamil","title":"IKACOR / IKAKOR — verapamil","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-verapamil","itemType":"medication","title":"IKACOR / IKAKOR — verapamil","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which target and site explain verapamil’s direct effects on cardiac conduction and contractility?","choices":[{"text":"L-type calcium channels predominantly in arteriolar smooth muscle","explanation":"This mainly vascular description does not identify the cardiac AV-node/myocardial site needed here.","id":"A"},{"text":"Fast sodium channels in the AV node and myocardium","explanation":"Verapamil’s relevant target is the L-type calcium channel rather than the fast sodium channel.","id":"B"},{"text":"L-type calcium channels in the AV node and myocardium","explanation":"Verapamil blocks these calcium channels, slowing AV conduction and myocardial activity.","id":"C"},{"text":"Potassium channels in the AV node and myocardium","explanation":"Verapamil’s relevant channel target is calcium rather than potassium.","id":"D"}],"answerKey":"C","point":"Identify L-type calcium-channel blockade in the AV node and myocardium.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does IKACOR / IKAKOR — verapamil belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"ACE inhibitor","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker; AV-node-slowing antiarrhythmic/antianginal."},{"id":"B","text":"Non-dihydropyridine CCB","explanation":"Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker; AV-node-slowing antiarrhythmic/antianginal."},{"id":"C","text":"Dihydropyridine CCB","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker; AV-node-slowing antiarrhythmic/antianginal."},{"id":"D","text":"Uroselective alpha-1 blocker","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker; AV-node-slowing antiarrhythmic/antianginal."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::drug-class"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which cardiovascular problem can fit an authorized verapamil regimen after rhythm and contraindication review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Selected supraventricular rate control with preserved perfusion","explanation":"Verapamil slows AV conduction and may be used in carefully selected supraventricular arrhythmias."},{"id":"B","text":"Unstable wide-complex tachycardia needing immediate electrical therapy","explanation":"Verapamil may cause serious deterioration in ventricular-origin wide-complex tachycardia."},{"id":"C","text":"Atrial fibrillation with known accessory-pathway pre-excitation","explanation":"AV-nodal blockade can be dangerous in pre-excited atrial fibrillation."},{"id":"D","text":"Symptomatic high-grade AV block with a slow escape rhythm","explanation":"Verapamil may worsen AV block rather than correct it."}],"answerKey":"A","point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which new pattern during IV verapamil most closely matches its important cardiac and circulatory adverse effects?","choices":[{"text":"Tachycardia, AV block and significant hypotension","explanation":"AV block and hypotension fit the risk profile, but bradycardia is the expected cardiac-slowing effect in this comparison.","id":"A"},{"text":"Bradycardia, AV block and significant hypotension","explanation":"Verapamil can depress AV conduction and myocardial activity while lowering BP.","id":"B"},{"text":"Bradycardia, accelerated AV conduction and significant hypotension","explanation":"Verapamil slows AV conduction rather than accelerating it.","id":"C"},{"text":"Bradycardia, AV block and significant hypertension","explanation":"The important pressure-lowering adverse effect is hypotension rather than hypertension.","id":"D"}],"answerKey":"B","point":"Recognize bradycardia, AV block and significant hypotension as verapamil adverse effects.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--bradycardia","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::bradycardia"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which bowel change is a common adverse effect of oral verapamil?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Watery stools with increased bowel frequency","explanation":"Diarrhea is not the characteristic effect sought."},{"id":"B","text":"Bloody stools with unchanged bowel frequency","explanation":"Bleeding needs assessment and is not routine verapamil constipation."},{"id":"C","text":"Pale stools with unchanged bowel frequency","explanation":"Pale stool is not the characteristic motility effect sought."},{"id":"D","text":"Hard stools with reduced bowel frequency","explanation":"Constipation is a common oral verapamil effect."}],"answerKey":"D","point":"Recognize oral verapamil constipation.","id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--verapamil_constipation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::verapamil_constipation"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"stem":"For a non-elderly adult receiving the initial IV verapamil dose, what is the minimum injection time? Enter the number in minutes.","format":"numeric-entry","answer":2,"unit":"minutes","tolerance":0,"explanation":"Give the initial IV injection over at least 2 minutes in non-elderly adults, with continuous ECG/BP monitoring. Gaza MOH’s injectable guide and current injection labeling agree. This is an administration time, not a dose.","point":"The taught IV administration minimum is 2 minutes; this is not an oral-product instruction.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--slow_iv","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::slow_iv"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which pair must be monitored continuously during IV verapamil administration?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Oxygen saturation and respiratory rate","explanation":"These do not replace the ECG/BP monitoring requirement."},{"id":"B","text":"ECG and body temperature","explanation":"This omits continuous blood-pressure monitoring."},{"id":"C","text":"Blood pressure and capillary glucose","explanation":"This omits the required continuous ECG observation."},{"id":"D","text":"ECG and blood pressure","explanation":"IV verapamil can impair AV conduction and lower pressure; both need continuous observation."}],"point":"Recall the required monitoring during IV verapamil rather than receiving it as a stem hint.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--verapamil_iv_monitoring","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::verapamil_iv_monitoring"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"IV verapamil is ordered shortly after an IV propranolol dose. What should happen before giving verapamil?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Give the dose slowly because the administration rate removes the interaction","explanation":"Slowing administration does not remove the contraindicated overlap."},{"id":"B","text":"Give the dose if the current BP is high enough","explanation":"A satisfactory current BP does not remove the conduction and contractility risk."},{"id":"C","text":"Withhold the dose and urgently clarify the contraindicated overlap","explanation":"Close IV beta-blocker and IV verapamil administration can add myocardial and AV-conduction depression; the referenced injection labeling contraindicates this overlap."},{"id":"D","text":"Give the dose through a second IV line to avoid the interaction","explanation":"Separate IV lines do not prevent a systemic pharmacodynamic interaction."}],"point":"Recognize the contraindicated close administration of IV verapamil and an IV beta blocker.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--verapamil_iv_beta","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::verapamil_iv_beta"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient in sinus rhythm is prescribed oral verapamil. Which additional therapeutic role can explain the order?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dissolving a coronary thrombus","explanation":"Verapamil is not a thrombolytic."},{"id":"B","text":"Preventing platelet aggregation","explanation":"Verapamil does not substitute for antiplatelet therapy."},{"id":"C","text":"Removing excess extracellular fluid","explanation":"Verapamil is not a diuretic."},{"id":"D","text":"Reducing angina symptoms","explanation":"Oral verapamil has an antianginal role independent of a current arrhythmia."}],"answerKey":"D","point":"Recall the antianginal role of verapamil.","id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--verapamil_antianginal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::verapamil_antianginal"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"During IV verapamil, a patient develops new symptomatic bradycardia with marked hypotension. What is the best immediate nursing response?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Stop administration and urgently escalate while supporting circulation","explanation":"This clinically important deterioration requires stopping further exposure and urgent assessment/support."},{"id":"B","text":"Finish administration and then seek urgent assessment","explanation":"Completing the dose adds exposure despite clinically important deterioration."},{"id":"C","text":"Pause until the next routine observation, then decide whether to report","explanation":"Waiting for routine observations delays necessary escalation."},{"id":"D","text":"Continue at half the rate and reassess after completion","explanation":"Reducing the rate does not justify continuing during symptomatic bradycardia and hypotension."}],"point":"Respond promptly to symptomatic cardiac/circulatory deterioration.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--verapamil_deterioration_action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::verapamil_deterioration_action"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"For an older adult recei