 the contraindicated close administration of IV verapamil and an IV beta blocker.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--verapamil_iv_beta","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::verapamil_iv_beta"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient in sinus rhythm is prescribed oral verapamil. Which additional therapeutic role can explain the order?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dissolving a coronary thrombus","explanation":"Verapamil is not a thrombolytic."},{"id":"B","text":"Preventing platelet aggregation","explanation":"Verapamil does not substitute for antiplatelet therapy."},{"id":"C","text":"Removing excess extracellular fluid","explanation":"Verapamil is not a diuretic."},{"id":"D","text":"Reducing angina symptoms","explanation":"Oral verapamil has an antianginal role independent of a current arrhythmia."}],"answerKey":"D","point":"Recall the antianginal role of verapamil.","id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--verapamil_antianginal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::verapamil_antianginal"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"During IV verapamil, a patient develops new symptomatic bradycardia with marked hypotension. What is the best immediate nursing response?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Stop administration and urgently escalate while supporting circulation","explanation":"This clinically important deterioration requires stopping further exposure and urgent assessment/support."},{"id":"B","text":"Finish administration and then seek urgent assessment","explanation":"Completing the dose adds exposure despite clinically important deterioration."},{"id":"C","text":"Pause until the next routine observation, then decide whether to report","explanation":"Waiting for routine observations delays necessary escalation."},{"id":"D","text":"Continue at half the rate and reassess after completion","explanation":"Reducing the rate does not justify continuing during symptomatic bradycardia and hypotension."}],"point":"Respond promptly to symptomatic cardiac/circulatory deterioration.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--verapamil_deterioration_action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::verapamil_deterioration_action"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"For an older adult receiving IV verapamil, what minimum injection duration is recommended?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"3 minutes","explanation":"Give over at least 3 minutes in older patients; Gaza MOH’s injectable guide and the reviewed current injection label agree."},{"id":"B","text":"5 minutes","explanation":"A longer duration may be ordered, but the labeled minimum asked for is 3 minutes."},{"id":"C","text":"1 minute","explanation":"This is too rapid for the older-patient instruction."},{"id":"D","text":"2 minutes","explanation":"This is the general initial adult minimum, not the slower older-patient instruction."}],"point":"Recall the slower minimum IV time in older patients.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--verapamil_older_iv_time","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::verapamil_older_iv_time"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"During ongoing oral verapamil treatment with digoxin, which concentration change and monitoring approach best fits their interaction?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Digoxin may rise; follow blood pressure without drug-level review","explanation":"The expected direction fits, but blood-pressure observation alone omits digoxin concentration and conduction review."},{"id":"B","text":"Digoxin may rise; review its serum level and AV conduction","explanation":"Verapamil can increase digoxin exposure; serum concentrations and cardiac conduction need review."},{"id":"C","text":"Digoxin may fall; follow blood pressure without drug-level review","explanation":"This reverses the expected concentration effect and omits the relevant monitoring."},{"id":"D","text":"Digoxin may fall; review its serum level and AV conduction","explanation":"Monitoring is relevant, but verapamil can increase rather than predictably lower digoxin exposure."}],"point":"Identify the AV-node and concentration interaction requiring digoxin review.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--verapamil_digoxin_review","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::verapamil_digoxin_review"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Verapamil and a beta-blocker overlap in a patient who develops dizziness and a slow pulse. Which mechanism best explains the concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Additive vasodilation with compensatory tachycardia","explanation":"Vasodilation does not explain the slow pulse and overlapping AV-node effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Increased preload with compensatory bradycardia","explanation":"The relevant interaction is conduction slowing, not a primary increase in preload."},{"id":"C","text":"Additive slowing of AV conduction","explanation":"Both can slow conduction and contribute to bradycardia or block."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduced absorption of both cardiac medicines","explanation":"Reduced exposure does not explain increased cardiac slowing."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Combining AV-node-slowing drugs can compound bradycardia or block.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--avnode_combination","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::avnode_combination"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":13,"correctionRevision":"gaza-administration-reference-20261109","model":"comprehensive-v1"},{"id":"ccu-verapamil--21-day","itemId":"ccu-verapamil","itemType":"medication","title":"IKACOR / IKAKOR — verapamil","stage":"21-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false