ration and conduction review."},{"id":"B","text":"Digoxin may rise; review its serum level and AV conduction","explanation":"Verapamil can increase digoxin exposure; serum concentrations and cardiac conduction need review."},{"id":"C","text":"Digoxin may fall; follow blood pressure without drug-level review","explanation":"This reverses the expected concentration effect and omits the relevant monitoring."},{"id":"D","text":"Digoxin may fall; review its serum level and AV conduction","explanation":"Monitoring is relevant, but verapamil can increase rather than predictably lower digoxin exposure."}],"point":"Identify the AV-node and concentration interaction requiring digoxin review.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--verapamil_digoxin_review","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::verapamil_digoxin_review"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Verapamil and a beta-blocker overlap in a patient who develops dizziness and a slow pulse. Which mechanism best explains the concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Additive vasodilation with compensatory tachycardia","explanation":"Vasodilation does not explain the slow pulse and overlapping AV-node effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Increased preload with compensatory bradycardia","explanation":"The relevant interaction is conduction slowing, not a primary increase in preload."},{"id":"C","text":"Additive slowing of AV conduction","explanation":"Both can slow conduction and contribute to bradycardia or block."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduced absorption of both cardiac medicines","explanation":"Reduced exposure does not explain increased cardiac slowing."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Combining AV-node-slowing drugs can compound bradycardia or block.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-verapamil--comprehensive--avnode_combination","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::avnode_combination"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":13,"correctionRevision":"gaza-administration-reference-20261109","model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":6,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"gaza-moh-injectables-2018-verapamil","title":"Gaza MOH: Guide of Injectable Medications in Governmental Hospitals (January 2018), p. 103","url":"https://www.moh.gov.ps/portal/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/دليل-اعطاء-الادوية-الوريدية-في-المستشفيات-5.pdf"},{"id":"label-ccu-verapamil","title":"\n       Verapamil \n       \n       Hydrochloride Injection, USP \n   ","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=1c993365-1b62-4524-e781-d307f28f63bc"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker; AV-node-slowing antiarrhythmic/antianginal.","mechanism":"Blocks L-type calcium channels in the AV node and myocardium.","use":"Selected supraventricular arrhythmias/rate control and antianginal therapy.","administration":"IV verapamil initial adult injection is given over at least 2 minutes; in older patients administer over at least 3 minutes, under continuous ECG and BP monitoring. Oral verapamil is a separate formulation.","monitor":"• Watch HR, rhythm and BP. • Report bradycardia, AV block or significant hypotension. • Constipation is common with oral therapy. • Do not give IV verapamil close to an IV beta-blocker dose (within a few hours): the referenced injection labeling contraindicates this combination because of additive myocardial and AV-conduction depression. Review other AV-node-slowing medicines, including digoxin. With ongoing oral verapamil, serum digoxin levels can rise; monitor levels and clinical toxicity under the prescribing plan.","why":"Verapamil slows AV-node conduction and myocardial activity; combining AV-node blockers can cause excessive depression.","availability":"YES. Palestinian essential-medicine data document 5-mg ampoules and 40-mg tablets. ED departmental stock confirmed by learner, 2 Nov 2026; verify actual injectable ampoule and ordered route."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker; AV-node-slowing antiarrhythmic/antianginal.","id":"section-0-68df9b3c76e17e43"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Blocks L-type calcium channels in the AV node and myocardium.","id":"section-1-b3e46f0a632678b1"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Selected supraventricular arrhythmias/rate control and antianginal therapy.","id":"section-2-7c9300ff2e130805"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"IV verapamil initial adult injection is given over at least 2 minutes; in older patients administer over at least 3 minutes, under continuous ECG and BP monitoring. Oral verapamil is a separate formulation.","id":"section-3-33632c00936acc73"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Watch HR, rhythm and BP. • Report bradycardia, AV block or significant hypotension. • Constipation is common with oral therapy. • Do not give IV verapamil close to an IV beta-blocker dose (within a few hours): the referenced injection labeling contraindicates this combination because of additive myocardial and AV-conduction depression. Review other AV-node-slowing medicines, including digoxin. With ongoing oral verapamil, serum digoxin levels can rise; monitor levels and clinical toxicity under the prescribing plan.","id":"section-4-52daa7d19b24f9fd"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Verapamil slows AV-node conduction and myocardial activity; combining AV-node blockers can cause excessive depression.","id":"section-5-93b40cea196f98bd"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"YES. Palestinian essential-medicine data document 5-mg ampoules and 40-mg tablets. ED departmental stock confirmed by learner, 2 Nov 2026; verify actual injectable ampoule and ordered route.","id":"section-6-4c239de702ebd3ea"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ccu-verapamil--standard","itemId":"ccu-verapamil","itemType":"medication","title":"IKACOR / IKAKOR — verapamil","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of IKACOR / IKAKOR — verapamil?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Forms metabolites that damage microbial DNA","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Verapamil acts in the AV node and myocardium."},{"id":"B","text":"Enhances antithrombin activity, predominantly against Xa","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Verapamil acts in the AV node and myocardium."},{"id":"C","text":"Supplies rapidly available glucose","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Verapamil acts in the AV node and myocardium."},{"id":"D","text":"Slows AV-nodal calcium-dependent conduction","explanation":"Verapamil acts in the AV node and myocardium."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-verapamil--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::principal-action"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does IKACOR / IKAKOR — verapamil belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"ACE inhibitor","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker; AV-node-slowing antiarrhythmic/antianginal."},{"id":"B","text":"Dihydropyridine CCB","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker; AV-node-slowing antiarrhythmic/antianginal."},{"id":"C","text":"Non-dihydropyridine CCB","explanation":"Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker; AV-node-slowing antiarrhythmic/antianginal."},{"id":"D","text":"Uroselective alpha-1 blocker","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker; AV-node-slowing antiarrhythmic/antianginal."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-verapamil--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::drug-class"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of IKACOR / IKAKOR — verapamil?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Replacement of circulating glucose in severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Selected supraventricular rate control or angina treatment","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Selected supraventricular arrhythmias/rate control and antianginal therapy."},{"id":"C","text":"Acute reversal of opioid respiratory toxicity","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-verapamil--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-verapamil::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient becomes light-headed on standing after IKACOR / IKAKOR — verapamil. Which paired assessment best checks the suspected adverse effect?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Postural BP and associated symptoms","explanation":"A positional pressure fall can account for the light-headedness."},{"id":"B","text":"Visual acuity and accommodation symptoms","explanation":"These evaluate visual effects rather