 muscle injury."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Severe muscle symptoms with dark urine raise concern for myopathy/rhabdomyolysis.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-atorvastatin--7-day--statin_muscle","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-atorvastatin::statin_muscle"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-atorvastatin--advanced--statin_muscle","answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient stable on TROVAN / LIPIDEX / LIPDIX — atorvastatin develops adverse symptoms after another medicine starts. Which comparison best tests an interaction-related explanation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The presenting symptoms against the original indication alone","explanation":"The original indication does not account for all new drug-related effects."},{"id":"B","text":"The current dose against a usual adult dose in isolation","explanation":"A usual dose may become unsafe when interacting exposure changes."},{"id":"C","text":"New exposures and their timing against the prior stable regimen","explanation":"The exposure change must be linked to onset and known interaction effects."},{"id":"D","text":"The last normal assessment against the current symptoms alone","explanation":"This establishes change but omits the exposure responsible for it."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"New interacting medicines or illness can alter drug safety.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-atorvastatin--7-day--interaction_review","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-atorvastatin::interaction_review"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-atorvastatin--advanced--interaction_review","answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":5}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ccu-furosemide","title":"LASIX — furosemide","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-furosemide","itemType":"medication","title":"LASIX — furosemide","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which renal target and site match furosemide?","choices":[{"text":"Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in the proximal tubule","explanation":"The transporter is associated with furosemide, but the renal site is the thick ascending limb rather than the proximal tubule.","id":"A"},{"text":"Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in the thick ascending loop of Henle","explanation":"Furosemide inhibits this transporter, producing loop diuresis.","id":"B"},{"text":"Na-Cl cotransporter in the thick ascending loop of Henle","explanation":"The loop site is relevant, but furosemide inhibits Na-K-2Cl rather than the thiazide-sensitive Na-Cl cotransporter.","id":"C"},{"text":"Mineralocorticoid receptor in the thick ascending loop of Henle","explanation":"Furosemide acts on the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter, not the mineralocorticoid receptor.","id":"D"}],"answerKey":"B","point":"Locate furosemide action at the Na-K-2Cl transporter in the thick ascending loop of Henle.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which diuretic class does furosemide belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Thiazide diuretic","explanation":"Thiazides act at a different nephron target."},{"id":"B","text":"Loop diuretic","explanation":"Furosemide is a loop diuretic."},{"id":"C","text":"Potassium-sparing diuretic","explanation":"Furosemide can increase potassium loss."},{"id":"D","text":"Osmotic diuretic","explanation":"Furosemide blocks tubular salt transport rather than acting as an osmotic agent."}],"answerKey":"B","point":"Recall furosemide classification.","id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which assessment most closely matches a clinical reason to give prescribed IV furosemide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Severe hypotension with dehydration and no pulmonary congestion","explanation":"Additional diuresis can worsen volume depletion and hypotension."},{"id":"B","text":"Oliguria caused by a mechanical urinary obstruction","explanation":"Treat the urinary obstruction; furosemide does not relieve mechanical outflow blockage."},{"id":"C","text":"Pulmonary congestion with fluid overload in acute heart failure","explanation":"IV loop diuretic treatment can relieve volume-overload-associated pulmonary congestion."},{"id":"D","text":"Low cardiac output from shock with depleted intravascular volume","explanation":"Diuresis is not a substitute for restoring volume and perfusion in hypovolemic shock."}],"answerKey":"C","point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"stem":"Which change best supports a beneficial decongestive response to furosemide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Less urine with increasing edema","explanation":"This does not support the intended decongestive response."},{"id":"B","text":"Easier breathing with fewer crackles and less edema","explanation":"Improvement should include the clinical overload picture, not urine alone."},{"id":"C","text":"A BP fall with increasing confusion","explanation":"This may indicate poor perfusion rather than uncomplicated benefit."},{"id":"D","text":"More orthopnea with increasing crackles","explanation":"This suggests worsening pulmonary congestion."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the clinical response of reduced congestion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--decongestion","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::decongestion"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which pair best follows renal function during furosemide treatment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Creatinine trend and stool-frequency trend","explanation":"Stool frequency does not replace urine-output assessment."},{"id":"B","text":"Urine-output trend and hemoglobin trend","explanation":"Hemoglobin does not replace the renal-function measurement."},{"id":"C","text":"Creatinine trend and urine-output trend","explanation":"Together these follow renal function and output."},{"id":"D","text":"Daily weight and serum-potassium trend","explanation":"Both are useful during diuresis but do not replace this renal-function/output pair."}],"answerKey":"C","point":"Follow creatinine and urine-output trends.","id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--renal_monitor","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::renal_monitor"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"stem":"Which new symptom during LASIX — furosemide therapy specifically warrants review for ototoxicity?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Metallic taste or mild dyspepsia","explanation":"These are a different adverse-effect pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Dry mouth or urinary hesitancy","explanation":"These suggest an antimuscarinic pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Ankle soreness or tendon tenderness","explanation":"These suggest a different medication warning."},{"id":"D","text":"Tinnitus or hearing change","explanation":"These can be auditory toxicity signals."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"New tinnitus or hearing change can indicate treatment-associated ototoxicity.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--ototoxicity","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::ototoxicity"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"For a controlled high-dose IV furosemide infusion in an adult, what is the referenced maximum rate? Enter the number in mg/min.","format":"numeric-entry","answer":4,"unit":"mg/min","tolerance":0,"explanation":"High-dose parenteral therapy should avoid rates above 4 mg/min; this is not a default dose.","point":"High-dose parenteral therapy should avoid rates above 4 mg/min; this is not a default dose.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--slow_iv","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::slow_iv"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"stem":"Why may an authorized routine oral furosemide schedule favor earlier daytime doses?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Less sleep disruption from nocturia","explanation":"The timing reduces inconvenient overnight diuresis."},{"id":"B","text":"Less potassium loss at an identical total dose","explanation":"Time of day does not remove the need to monitor potassium."},{"id":"C","text":"Faster conversion into a potassium-sparing metabolite","explanation":"Furosemide does not become a potassium-sparing diuretic."},{"id":"D","text":"Greater platelet inhibition during daylight","explanation":"Furosemide is not an antiplatelet drug."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Routine oral diuretic timing can reduce nocturia.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--diuretic_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::diuretic_timing"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which sodium/water and BP pattern can follow excessive furosemide diuresis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Sodium/water depletion with a fall in BP","explanation":"Excessive diuresis can deplete sodium and intravascular volume and produce hypotension."},{"id":"B","text":"Sodium/water retention with a fall in BP","explanation":"Retention is not the direct excessive-diuresis effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Sodium/water retention with a predictable BP rise","explanation":"This does not describe excessive loop diuresis."},{"id":"D","text":"Sodium/water depletion with a predictable BP rise","explanation":"A BP rise is not the expected volume-depletion response."}],"point":"Recognize sodium/water depletion and hypotension as risks of excessive furosemide diuresis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--furosemide_volume_sodium","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::furosemide_volume_sodium"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving repeated furosemide doses has potassium, magnesium and creatinine scheduled. Which additional electrolyte measurement completes the recommended panel?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Serum ionized calcium","explanation":"Calcium assessment does not replace the missing sodium measurement."},{"id":"B","text":"Serum phosphate","explanation":"Phosphate does not replace the missing sodium measurement."},{"id":"C","text":"Serum bicarbonate alone","explanation":"Bicarbonate does not replace direct sodium measurement."},{"id":"D","text":"Serum sodium","explanation":"Sodium is part of the required electrolyte surveillance during diuresis."}],"point":"Include serum sodium in electrolyte surveillance.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--furosemide_sodium_monitor","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::furosemide_sodium_monitor"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which change in furosemide administration increases the risk of ototoxicity?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Giving a routine oral dose earlier in the day","explanation":"Earlier oral timing helps limit nocturia and is not the administration risk sought."},{"id":"B","text":"Using the prescribed controlled IV infusion rate","explanation":"Controlled delivery avoids unnecessary rapid exposure."},{"id":"C","text":"Giving a large IV dose more rapidly","explanation":"Large doses and rapid injection increase the risk of ototoxicity."},{"id":"D","text":"Maintaining the ordered dose with slower IV delivery","explanation":"Slower delivery does not create the rapid-injection risk sought."}],"point":"Connect rapid/high-dose IV administration with ototoxicity risk.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--furosemide_rapid_ototoxicity","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::furosemide_rapid_ototoxicity"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which delivery method is appropriate for prescribed high-dose parenteral furosemide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Controlled infusion within the prescribed rate limit","explanation":"High-dose parenteral therapy requires controlled delivery within the authorized rate."},{"id":"B","text":"A free-flow infusion adjusted only when symptoms occur","explanation":"Symptom-triggered adjustment does not provide the required rate control."},{"id":"C","text":"A rapid IV push followed by a slow saline flush","explanation":"A slow flush does not make the preceding rapid drug injection a controlled infusion."},{"id":"D","text":"Intermittent rapid pushes separated by observation pauses","explanation":"Pauses do not control the rate of each rapid injection."}],"point":"Select controlled infusion for high-dose parenteral therapy.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--furosemide_controlled_delivery","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::furosemide_controlled_delivery"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"For a routine adult 20–40-mg IV dose of Hikma furosemide 10 mg/mL, which injection duration follows its US labeling?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"1–2 minutes","explanation":"This is the routine adult IV duration specified by the identified label."},{"id":"B","text":"5–10 seconds","explanation":"This is substantially faster than the labeled routine injection."},{"id":"C","text":"15–30 minutes","explanation":"A longer infusion may be separately prescribed, but it is not the routine injection duration asked for here."},{"id":"D","text":"15–30 seconds","explanation":"This is faster than the labeled 1–2-minute routine injection."}],"point":"Recall the specified routine adult IV time for the identified product.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--furosemide_routine_iv_time","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::furosemide_routine_iv_time"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient on furosemide develops new PVCs after substantial diuresis. Which electrolyte pair is especially important to check?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Potassium and phosphate","explanation":"Potassium matters, but magnesium is the second key electrolyte in this comparison."},{"id":"B","text":"Magnesium and phosphate","explanation":"Magnesium matters, but this omits potassium."},{"id":"C","text":"Sodium and chloride","explanation":"Both can change during diuresis, but this pair omits the potassium/magnesium arrhythmia concern."},{"id":"D","text":"Potassium and magnesium","explanation":"Loss of either may contribute to arrhythmia during diuresis."}],"answerKey":"D","point":"Identify both potassium and magnesium monitoring for post-diuresis arrhythmia.","id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--low_k_mg","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::low_k_mg"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]}],"questionCount":14,"correctionRevision":"closed-source-review-20261107","model":"comprehensive-v1"},{"id":"ccu-furosemide--7-day","itemId":"ccu-furosemide","itemType":"medication","title":"LASIX — furosemide","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswe