oints":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::ototoxicity"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"For a controlled high-dose IV furosemide infusion in an adult, what is the referenced maximum rate? Enter the number in mg/min.","format":"numeric-entry","answer":4,"unit":"mg/min","tolerance":0,"explanation":"High-dose parenteral therapy should avoid rates above 4 mg/min; this is not a default dose.","point":"High-dose parenteral therapy should avoid rates above 4 mg/min; this is not a default dose.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--slow_iv","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::slow_iv"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"stem":"Why may an authorized routine oral furosemide schedule favor earlier daytime doses?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Less sleep disruption from nocturia","explanation":"The timing reduces inconvenient overnight diuresis."},{"id":"B","text":"Less potassium loss at an identical total dose","explanation":"Time of day does not remove the need to monitor potassium."},{"id":"C","text":"Faster conversion into a potassium-sparing metabolite","explanation":"Furosemide does not become a potassium-sparing diuretic."},{"id":"D","text":"Greater platelet inhibition during daylight","explanation":"Furosemide is not an antiplatelet drug."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Routine oral diuretic timing can reduce nocturia.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--comprehensive--diuretic_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::diuretic_timing"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which sodium/water and BP pattern can follow excessive furosemide diuresis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Sodium/water depletion with a fall in BP","explanation":"Excessive diuresis can deplete sodium and intravascular volume and produce hypotension."},{"id":"B","text":"Sodium/water retention with a fall in BP","explanation":"Retention is not the direct excessive-diuresis ef