k urine output and congestion. • Check BP. • Follow K+, Mg2+, Na+ and renal function. • Report tinnitus/hearing change, especially with large/rapid IV doses. • Routine oral doses are often given earlier in the day to reduce nocturia.","id":"section-4-7bc6c34f9ef4e3ef"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Loop diuresis removes sodium/water rapidly but can deplete electrolytes and volume.","id":"section-5-5405bfaf14e369f7"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"YES. Palestinian essential-medicine data document a 20-mg ampoule, 40-mg tablet and a higher-strength injectable presentation. ED shelf/drawer label photographed, 2 Nov 2026. This is a named compartment, not an independently audited stock count; check the package before use.","id":"section-6-ef8893c12d12d34d"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ccu-furosemide--standard","itemId":"ccu-furosemide","itemType":"medication","title":"LASIX — furosemide","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of LASIX — furosemide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Furosemide promotes salt and water loss, reducing congestion."},{"id":"B","text":"Inhibits Na-K-2Cl transport in the ascending loop","explanation":"Furosemide promotes salt and water loss, reducing congestion."},{"id":"C","text":"Receptor blockade and sodium loss","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Furosemide promotes salt and water loss, reducing congestion."},{"id":"D","text":"Inhibits hepatic HMG-CoA reductase","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Furosemide promotes salt and water loss, reducing congestion."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::principal-action"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does LASIX — furosemide belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Insulin","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Loop diuretic."},{"id":"B","text":"Potassium-sparing diuretic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Loop diuretic."},{"id":"C","text":"Loop diuretic","explanation":"Loop diuretic."},{"id":"D","text":"SGLT2 inhibitor","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Loop diuretic."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::drug-class"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of LASIX — furosemide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Treatment of oral fungal lesions","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Reversal of vitamin-K-antagonist activity","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Suppression of bacterial cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Relief of congestion and fluid overload","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Pulmonary/peripheral congestion and fluid overload, including selected acute heart-failure patients."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which change best supports a beneficial decongestive response to furosemide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Less urine with increasing edema","explanation":"This does not support the intended decongestive response."},{"id":"B","text":"A BP fall with increasing confusion","explanation":"This may indicate poor perfusion rather than uncomplicated benefit."},{"id":"C","text":"More orthopnea with increasing crackles","explanation":"This suggests worsening pulmonary congestion."},{"id":"D","text":"Easier breathing with fewer crackles and less edema","explanation":"Improvement should include the clinical overload picture, not urine alone."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the clinical response of reduced congestion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--standard--decongestion","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::decongestion"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient becomes light-headed on standing after LASIX — furosemide. Which paired assessment best checks the suspected adverse effect?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Postural BP and associated symptoms","explanation":"A positional pressure fall can account for the light-headedness."},{"id":"B","text":"Bowel frequency and abdominal distension","explanation":"These investigate a gastrointestinal effect rather than postural circulatory tolerance."},{"id":"C","text":"Visual acuity and accommodation symptoms","explanation":"These evaluate visual effects rather than establish a postural pressure fall."},{"id":"D","text":"Resting temperature and infection symptoms","explanation":"These investigate infection rather than the suspected pressure effect."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize symptomatic hypotension during treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--standard--hypotension","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::hypotension"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which trend is most directly relevant to the renal safety monitoring described for LASIX — furosemide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Troponin and chest-pain trends","explanation":"These assess myocardial injury rather than directly establish renal status."},{"id":"B","text":"Temperature and neutrophil-count trends","explanation":"These primarily assess infection or blood-cell changes."},{"id":"C","text":"Creatinine and urine-output trends","explanation":"These assess kidney function and renal perfusion in clinical context."},{"id":"D","text":"INR and puncture-site bleeding trends","explanation":"These assess anticoagulation and bleeding rather than renal function."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Monitor renal function when kidney injury or altered treatment safety is relevant.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--standard--renal_monitor","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::renal_monitor"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which new symptom during LASIX — furosemide therapy specifically warrants review for ototoxicity?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Metallic taste or mild dyspepsia","explanation":"These are a different adverse-effect pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Dry mouth or urinary hesitancy","explanation":"These suggest an antimuscarinic pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Tinnitus or hearing change","explanation":"These can be auditory toxicity signals."},{"id":"D","text":"Ankle soreness or tendon tenderness","explanation":"These suggest a different medication warning."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"New tinnitus or hearing change can indicate treatment-associated ototoxicity.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--standard--ototoxicity","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::ototoxicity"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A LASIX — furosemide injection order is valid, but the minimum administration time is unclear. Which source should resolve preparation and rate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The IV rate previously used for a different drug class","explanation":"Another drug’s administration rate is not transferable."},{"id":"B","text":"The exact product instructions and authorized local protocol","explanation":"Concentration, formulation, and indication can change administration requirements."},{"id":"C","text":"The instructions for an oral product of the same name","explanation":"Oral instructions do not establish an IV administration rate."},{"id":"D","text":"The instructions for a similar-looking vial on the trolley","explanation":"Appearance does not establish an equivalent product or rate."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"IV administration speed follows the exact product and indication.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--standard--slow_iv","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::slow_iv"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Why may an authorized routine oral furosemide schedule favor earlier daytime doses?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Less potassium loss at an identical total dose","explanation":"Time of day does not remove the need to monitor potassium."},{"id":"B","text":"Less sleep disruption from nocturia","explanation":"The timing reduces inconvenient overnight diuresis."},{"id":"C","text":"Faster conversion into a potassium-sparing metabolite","explanation":"Furosemide does not become a potassium-sparing diuretic."},{"id":"D","text":"Greater platelet inhibition during daylight","explanation":"Furosemide is not an antiplatelet drug."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Routine oral diuretic timing can reduce nocturia.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--standard--diuretic_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::diuretic_timing"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which sodium/water and BP pattern can follow excessive furosemide diuresis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Sodium/wate