:"Which delivery method is appropriate for prescribed high-dose parenteral furosemide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A rapid IV push followed by a slow saline flush","explanation":"A slow flush does not make the preceding rapid drug injection a controlled infusion."},{"id":"B","text":"Controlled infusion within the prescribed rate limit","explanation":"High-dose parenteral therapy requires controlled delivery within the authorized rate."},{"id":"C","text":"A free-flow infusion adjusted only when symptoms occur","explanation":"Symptom-triggered adjustment does not provide the required rate control."},{"id":"D","text":"Intermittent rapid pushes separated by observation pauses","explanation":"Pauses do not control the rate of each rapid injection."}],"point":"Select controlled infusion for high-dose parenteral therapy.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-furosemide--7-day--furosemide_controlled_delivery","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::furosemide_controlled_delivery"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-furosemide--standard--furosemide_controlled_delivery","answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"For a routine adult 20–40-mg IV dose of Hikma furosemide 10 mg/mL, which injection duration follows its US labeling?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"15–30 minutes","explanation":"A longer infusion may be separately prescribed, but it is not the routine injection duration asked for here."},{"id":"B","text":"1–2 minutes","explanation":"This is the routine adult IV duration specified by the identified label."},{"id":"C","text":"5–10 seconds","explanation":"This is substantially faster than the labeled routine injection."},{"id":"D","text":"15–30 seconds","explanation":"This is faster than the labeled 1–2-minute routine injection."}],"point":"Recall the specified routine adult IV time for the identified product.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-furosemide--7-day--furosemide_routine_iv_time","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::furosemide_routine_iv_time"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-furosemide--standard--furosemide_routine_iv_time","answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient on furosemide develops new PVCs after substantial diuresis. Which laboratory pair is especially relevant?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"TSH and free thyroxine","explanation":"Thyroid status can affect rhythm but does not best match the immediate diuretic-loss context."},{"id":"B","text":"Albumin and bilirubin","explanation":"These do not directly address the electrolyte-related rhythm concern."},{"id":"C","text":"Potassium and magnesium","explanation":"Low levels can contribute to ventricular irritability after diuresis."},{"id":"D","text":"Amylase and lipase","explanation":"These assess a different organ system."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Loop-diuretic electrolyte losses can contribute to ectopy.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-furosemide--7-day--low_k_mg","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-furosemide::low_k_mg"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-furosemide--advanced--low_k_mg","answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":15}],"scoringExclusions":[{"learningPointId":"ccu-furosemide::hypotension","action":"omit","reason":"Context-free shared template: the answer is supplied by the symptom or asks for a general product-reference rule. Omit from drug-specific scoring; do not credit this as coverage of the underlying drug-specific administration or BP fact.","unresolvedCoverage":true}]},{"itemId":"ccu-dapagliflozin","title":"FARIGM — dapagliflozin","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-dapagliflozin--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-dapagliflozin","itemType":"medication","title":"FARIGM — dapagliflozin","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":1},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafety