est establishes the immediate response?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Repeat tablet count without a glucose measurement","explanation":"Remaining supply does not show the treatment response."},{"id":"B","text":"Repeat glucose with consciousness and symptoms","explanation":"Biochemical and clinical response should be assessed together."},{"id":"C","text":"Repeat pain score without symptom review","explanation":"This does not establish resolution of the glucose problem."},{"id":"D","text":"Repeat BP without a glucose measurement","explanation":"BP alone cannot establish glucose correction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recheck glucose and the patient after glucose-lowering or rescue treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-correction-insulin--7-day--glucose_recheck","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-correction-insulin::glucose_recheck"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-correction-insulin--standard--glucose_recheck","answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"An insulin order states units, but the supplied container has a different concentration from the one usually used. Which principle prevents a dosing error?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The previous patient’s injection volume defines this patient’s dose","explanation":"The dose and device must be verified against the current order."},{"id":"B","text":"The same injection volume gives the same units across concentrations","explanation":"Units per mL change with concentration."},{"id":"C","text":"The same brand family establishes identical action and concentration","explanation":"Different products within a family can differ."},{"id":"D","text":"The same units may require a different volume or delivery device","explanation":"Identity, concentration, units, and product instructions must agree."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Verify insulin identity, concentration, and dose.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-correction-insulin--7-day--insulin_product","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-correction-insulin::in