y":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of NOREPINEPHRINE / NORADRENALINE?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduces angiotensin-II formation","explanation":"This describes ACE inhibitor. Norepinephrine supports vascular tone and pressure, with some beta1 activity."},{"id":"B","text":"Blocks angiotensin-II AT1 receptors","explanation":"This describes ARB. Norepinephrine supports vascular tone and pressure, with some beta1 activity."},{"id":"C","text":"Predominantly alpha1-mediated vasoconstriction","explanation":"Norepinephrine supports vascular tone and pressure, with some beta1 activity."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduces vagal slowing of the heart","explanation":"This describes Antimuscarinic. Norepinephrine supports vascular tone and pressure, with some beta1 activity."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-norepinephrine--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-norepinephrine::principal-action"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does NOREPINEPHRINE / NORADRENALINE belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Catecholamine","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Catecholamine vasopressor; predominantly alpha1 with beta1 activity."},{"id":"B","text":"Organic nitrate","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Catecholamine vasopressor; predominantly alpha1 with beta1 activity."},{"id":"C","text":"Inotrope","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Catecholamine vasopressor; predominantly alpha1 with beta1 activity."},{"id":"D","text":"Vasopressor","explanation":"Catecholamine vasopressor; predominantly alpha1 with beta1 activity."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-norepinephrine--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-norepinephrine::drug-class"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"An adult in septic shock remains hypotensive after initial fluid resuscitation. There is no evidence of concomitant cardiac dysfunction. Which infusion is the usual first-line vasopressor?","choices":[{"text":"Norepinephrine","explanation":"Norepinephrine is the usual first-line vasopressor in this setting.","id":"A"},{"text":"Epinephrine","explanation":"Epinephrine can be used in selected situations, such as inadequate MAP despite initial vasopressors.","id":"B"},{"text":"Vasopressin","explanation":"Vasopressin is typically added as norepinephrine requirements rise.","id":"C"},{"text":"Dopamine","explanation":"Dopamine has selected uses but is not the usual first-line vasopressor in adult septic shock.","id":"D"}],"point":"Norepinephrine is the usual first-line vasopressor in adult septic shock.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-norepinephrine--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-norepinephrine::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"stem":"Which feature is required for safe IV administration of NOREPINEPHRINE / NORADRENALINE?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Gravity flow without accounting for the prepared concentration","explanation":"Accurate controlled dosing depends on the concentration and delivery device."},{"id":"B","text":"Controlled infusion using the product-specific preparation and order","explanation":"Delivery and concentration must follow the authorized infusion regimen."},{"id":"C","text":"Undiluted rapid injection based on the ampoule’s full volume","explanation":"Container content is not a default patient bolus."},{"id":"D","text":"Intermittent manual boluses using the last infusion’s mL/hour","explanation":"An infusion rate cannot define a safe bolus."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use controlled, product-appropriate IV infusion rather than an improvised rapid bolus.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-norepinephrine--comprehensive--infusion_route","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-norepinephrine::infusion_route"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"An adult with septic shock needs urgent norepinephrine. Central access is being arranged, and a peripheral IV meets the unit’s vasopressor criteria. How should the nurse prepare to start the order?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Prepare the pump now and wait to start until central access is confirmed.","explanation":"Waiting would delay urgent treatment despite a suitable peripheral IV."},{"id":"B","text":"Use the peripheral IV for fluids and substitute dobutamine until central access is ready.","explanation":"Dobutamine does not replace needed vasopressor support."},{"id":"C","text":"Give a peripheral loading bolus while the continuous infusion is prepared.","explanation":"The shock order calls for a controlled continuous infusion."},{"id":"D","text":"Use the suitable peripheral IV now and follow the site-monitoring protocol.","explanation":"A suitable peripheral IV allows prompt treatment while access needs are reassessed."}],"point":"A suitable peripheral IV can be used for urgent initiation when central access would delay treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-norepinephrine--comprehensive--norepinephrine_peripheral_start","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-norepinephrine::norepinephrine_peripheral_start"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"The patient’s pressure has stabilized on norepinephrine and the team orders weaning. Which plan addresses the main immediate risk of abrupt withdrawal?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduce the rate in ordered steps and reassess blood pressure at each step.","explanation":"Abrupt withdrawal can cause marked hypotension."},{"id":"B","text":"Stop the infusion and use urine output to decide whether to restart.","explanation":"Urine output responds too slowly to detect immediate hypotension."},{"id"