on protocol (2026)","url":"https://www.uab.edu/medicine/surgery/images/Trauma_and_Acute_Care_Surgery/Protocols/uab-guideline-for-peripheral-iv-vasopressor-use.final.march-2026.pdf"},{"id":"review-label-ccu-norepinephrine","title":"DailyMed Norepinephrine bitartrate injection","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=5fbd0e3b-703b-412e-8b71-bd0a0dbf4500"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Catecholamine vasopressor; predominantly alpha1 with beta1 activity.","mechanism":"Alpha1-mediated vasoconstriction with some beta1 cardiac stimulation.","use":"Vasopressor for severe hypotension. In adult septic shock, norepinephrine is the usual first-line vasopressor. Cardiac dysfunction may change the choice.","administration":"For shock, infuse IV continuously on a pump. Verify the exact product, compatible diluent and standard concentration. A suitable peripheral IV can be used to start urgent treatment when central access would delay it. Follow the facility protocol for site checks and transition to central access.","monitor":"• Follow BP and perfusion closely during titration. • Inspect a peripheral IV frequently for pain, pallor, swelling, coolness or impaired flow. For suspected extravasation, stop the infusion through that IV, leave the catheter in place and avoid flushing. Escalate immediately for aspiration and treatment per protocol, and coordinate safe alternate access if pressure support is still needed. • Taper as ordered when discontinuing. Abrupt withdrawal can cause marked hypotension.","why":"Strong vasoconstriction raises systemic vascular resistance/MAP; extravasation can cause local ischemia.","availability":"YES. Palestinian essential-medicine data document 4-mg ampoules."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Catecholamine vasopressor; predominantly alpha1 with beta1 activity.","id":"section-0-7f7dc2e8353dc150"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Alpha1-mediated vasoconstriction with