ine_route","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-atropine::atropine_route"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-atropine--standard--atropine_route","answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which repeat-dose order matches the AHA adult bradycardia pathway after the initial atropine bolus?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Every 5–10 minutes as indicated, maximum total 3 mg","explanation":"The maximum fits, but the repeat interval is 3–5 minutes."},{"id":"B","text":"Every 3–5 minutes as indicated, maximum total 5 mg","explanation":"The interval fits, but 5 mg exceeds the maximum."},{"id":"C","text":"Every 3–5 minutes as indicated, maximum total 3 mg","explanation":"Count the initial bolus toward the 3 mg maximum."},{"id":"D","text":"Every 5–10 minutes as indicated, maximum total 5 mg","explanation":"Both the interval and maximum differ from the adult pathway."}],"point":"Repeat atropine every 3–5 minutes if indicated, with a 3 mg maximum cumulative dose.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-atropine--7-day--atropine_repeat_limit","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-atropine::atropine_repeat_limit"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-atropine--standard--atropine_repeat_limit","answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"After atropine, a patient remains hypotensive and confused with severe bradycardia. Which treatment category should the team prepare for along the bradycardia pathway?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"AV-node-blocking treatment to reduce the ventricular rate","explanation":"Further slowing can worsen the described compromise."},{"id":"B","text":"Defibrillation for the slow rhythm with a definite pulse","explanation":"The scenario does not describe VF or pulseless VT."},{"id":"C","text":"Pacing and/or authorized chronotropic support","explanation":"Persistent compromise requires escalation beyond ineffective atropine."},{"id":"D","text":"Synchronized cardioversion for the slow rhythm","explanation":"This treats selected unstable tachyarrhythmias, not the stated bradycardia."}],"form